WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:03.740
The Hulk has been a popular fixture in the
MCU for years.

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:08.340
But it wasn't until later films, like Thor:
Ragnarok, Infinity War and Endgame, that the

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.340
Hulk narrative was really let off the leash.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.160
Here's a run-down of the Hulk's hidden MCU
trilogy.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:18.440
Hulk kicked things off in the MCU back in
2008 with The Incredible Hulk.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.280
The story wasn't exactly a bad one, but it
wasn't a great one, either, especially considering

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.280
the caliber of so many of the Marvel movies
that came after it.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.300
Part of the issue with Hulk's non-starter
in the MCU was the fact that the movie picked

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.220
up partway into the story and proceeded to
go…kinda nowhere.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:39.120
The movie introduces Banner after he's already
become the Hulk, skipping right over his infamous

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.200
gamma radiation incident in the process.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:45.559
Of course, that doesn't automatically equate
to a negative, Captain Marvel also jumps right

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.540
into the story as it's already developing.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:52.239
The difference, however, is that Carol Danvers'
film circles back around to show how she got

00:00:52.239 --> 00:00:53.360
her superpowers.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:57.359
The Incredible Hulk simply charges ahead,
assuming everyone already knows about the

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.020
whole gamma radiation thing.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:03.500
Things still could have turned out alright
if the film had ended in an appropriate place,

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.340
but, once again, it manages to drop the ball.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:10.800
While the climactic fight against Abomination
is fun, the movie ends with Banner finally

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:15.220
coming to terms with Hulk…which would have
been great, if Marvel didn't then retcon it

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:16.220
in The Avengers.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:21.340
Sadly, a meandering storyline is just the
beginning of The Incredible Hulk's problems.

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:26.040
Another issue with the film is its excessively
serious nature, which contrasts starkly against

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:27.760
Iron Man's light and witty tone.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:32.600
Edward Norton's version of Bruce Banner is
sulky, serious, and spends most of the film

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:33.100
depressed.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:37.219
While the character is certainly an improvement
over the version seen in Ang Lee's Hulk, which

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:40.840
came out half a decade earlier, it just doesn't
fit the MCU's tone.

00:01:41.189 --> 00:01:45.340
The movie just feels like it belongs in an
entirely different universe than that of Tony

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:45.840
Stark.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:50.080
Another complication that surfaced was the
growing tension between Norton and Marvel

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:50.700
Studios.

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:54.860
That's not to say that Norton wasn't committed
to the role, on the contrary, the Academy

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:58.500
Award-nominated actor brought a lot of vision
and depth to the character.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:02.380
But when the rubber hit the road, things just
didn't fit well behind the scenes.

00:02:02.399 --> 00:02:06.790
In the ensuing fallout, the actor and the
studio parted ways, not on the most pleasant

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:09.700
of terms, and the Hulk found himself at a
crossroads.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:14.820
While characters like Spider-Man, the X-Men,
and the Fantastic Four have all had clear

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:19.071
cut rights issues over the years, many of
which have been ironed out over time, one

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:23.540
stubborn conundrum that has refused to resolve
itself is the question of the Hulk's rights.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:28.440
Putting it as simply as possible, the rights
to Hulk are kind of "split" between Marvel

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:29.140
and Universal.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.860
Marvel Studios is allowed to use the Hulk
character in its movies, a right they regained

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:38.820
back in 2005, after Universal failed to pull
the trigger on a sequel to Ang Lee's Hulk.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:43.760
However, Universal still retains the "right
of first refusal" for any solo Hulk film,

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.720
which allows them to distribute any Hulk movie
that's made.

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:50.940
That complicating little concern, along with
the fact that The Incredible Hulk performed

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:55.550
relatively poorly at the box office, pushed
Marvel to abandon any efforts to make another

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:56.180
Hulk movie.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:00.750
When everything was taken into consideration,
The Incredible Hulk 2 just wouldn't have been

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:02.940
worth the time and effort it would have required.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:08.180
While his first MCU foray seemed to peter
out after just one film, Hulk would eventually

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:09.460
get a second chance.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:13.740
Distribution rights may have made for tough
sledding with a Hulk solo movie, but over

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:18.580
the following years, Marvel rehabilitated
the Jade Giant by utilizing him as an effective

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:20.240
and interesting supporting character.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:25.180
This began with casting Mark Ruffalo in the
role, a wise move, considering the actor instantly

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:27.920
won over the hearts of the fans when he arrived
on screen.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:33.600
Banner's MCU storyline was also largely reset;
he was back to not getting along with Hulk

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:36.780
and was clearly scared of letting "the Other
Guy" get in the driver seat.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:41.709
The Banner/Hulk combo made a big splash with
audiences in The Avengers, leading to the

00:03:41.709 --> 00:03:45.470
decision to have Banner pop in for a quick
"counseling" session with Tony Stark in the

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:47.480
post-credits scene for Iron Man 3.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:52.040
Banner and Hulk showed up again in the second
Avengers film, in which they both play a major

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:54.620
role in bringing down Ultron's apocalyptic
threat.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:58.970
At that point in the franchise, however, Hulk
and Banner were still just supporting players

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:00.560
in an ever-growing universe.

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:05.659
It wasn't until 2017 that Ruffalo and Marvel
boss Kevin Feige had sat down to chat about

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:07.240
Hulk's future in the MCU.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:11.610
It was during this conversation that Ruffalo
and Feige decided to give the Hulk his own

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:13.280
so-called "special trilogy".

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:18.620
Feige and Ruffalo's conversation ended up
pinning Hulk down to a single storyline that

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:21.960
would be split up over the course of three
other characters' movies.

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:27.200
This plan sidestepped any distribution issues
by not casting Hulk as a main character in

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:31.450
any of the three films, but it still allowed
for plenty of time to create a character arc

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:35.300
with a meaningful impact, something Hulk hadn't
experienced up to that point.

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:40.180
Things kick off at the very end of Age of
Ultron, with Hulk flying off in the Avengers'

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:40.680
Quinjet.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:44.560
From there, he remains safely tucked away
in space as the events of a few more Marvel

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:49.110
films pass by, including Captain America:
Civil War, in which his old nemesis, General

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:52.040
Thaddeus Ross, makes a point of questioning
Hulk's whereabouts.

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:55.030
"The Avengers were formed to make the world
a safer place.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:56.840
I feel we've done that."

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:00.900
"Tell me, Captain, do you know where Thor
and Banner are right now?"

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:05.940
The Infinity Saga doesn't find Hulk in the
flesh again until Thor: Ragnarok, in which

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:10.420
the "Lord of Thunder" discovers his "Friend
from Work" on the garbage planet of Sakaar.

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:14.620
Hulk's presence throughout Ragnarok is the
perfect compliment to Thor's story.

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:18.650
But at the same time, it also serves as a
vehicle to propel his own story through the

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:20.600
first third of its "three-movie-arc."

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:25.220
After their electrifying first encounter in
the Grandmaster's arena, the two friends have

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:29.560
a long, enlightening, and downright hilarious
conversation that demonstrates a new side

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:30.200
to Hulk's character.

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:34.500
While he's still very rough around the edges,
the Big Guy has clearly matured during his

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:35.440
time on Sakaar.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:39.460
Over the course of their conversation, Hulk
speaks in fuller sentences and reveals his

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:41.640
attachment to his new, Banner-free life.

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:43.080
"Banner's friend!"

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:44.080
"I don't even like Banner.

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:46.460
He's all, 'I'm into numbers and science and
stuff.'"

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:52.180
Nevertheless, shortly afterward, the pair
visit the Quinjet, where a recording of Natasha

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:55.380
Romanoff helps Banner regain control for the
first time in years.

00:05:55.750 --> 00:06:00.840
From there, the good doctor joins the newly-minted
team of "Revengers" and heads off to Asgard

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:02.480
to help Thor unseat Hela.

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:06.780
It's on Asgard that the first portion of the
character's "trilogy" concludes.

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:11.640
In a movie filled with Hulk smashing, arguably
the most heroic moment comes when Banner willingly

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:16.520
allows Hulk to regain control, knowing he
may never come back, in order to save Asgard.

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:20.700
The film ends with the character still Hulked
out and living on the refugee ship with the

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:22.760
surviving Asgardians…headed for Earth.

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:27.880
Things quickly went south for the Hulk during
Infinity War, which was released the following

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:28.380
year.

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:33.940
The third Avengers film opens with the Asgardian
ship already overrun by Thanos, and Hulk ends

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:36.940
up right in the thick of it only a few minutes
into the film.

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:40.960
But this time, things don't go according to
plan, and the Green Machine ends up down for

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:43.720
the count after taking a few blows from his
savvy opponent.

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:48.520
At that point, Heimdall uses the last of his
strength to summon the Bifrost and send Hulk

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:52.280
hurtling back to Earth, where he arrives in
NYC, back in Banner form.

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:55.360
From then on, Hulk flat-out refuses to join
in the fight.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:59.840
Fans believed that Hulk would play some part
in the fight against Thanos, but the deliberate

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:03.810
withdrawal of the character after Infinity
War's opening scene ultimately makes his arc

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:04.980
that much more interesting.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:09.550
The truth is, Hulk had been little more than
a pawn in the Avengers' plans up until this

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:13.340
point, often only whipped out to save the
day when danger was about to strike.

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:17.240
His time on Sakaar gave him a taste of freedom,
and the Big Guy liked it.

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:20.060
Co-director Joe Russo explains it best.

00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:24.410
"People speculated whether there was some
fear on the Hulk's part about having to face

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:28.560
Thanos again, but I think ultimately what
it is is that he's tired of playing hero."

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:33.700
In the lead up to Infinity War, Mark Ruffalo
revealed in an interview that Bruce Banner

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:35.620
and the Hulk were "headed for a showdown."

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:39.750
While the hint seemed to point towards a violent
conclusion, the rest of Hulk's three-part

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:42.820
story actually ended up playing out in an
unexpected way.

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:47.780
For a character who was so often used as an
explosive plot device, or as a way to entertain

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:52.440
smash-happy fans, the pivotal moment for Hulk
and Banner ended up being relatively quiet.

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:55.480
Not only that, but it actually took place
off-screen.

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:59.860
When audiences caught up with Hulk again in
Endgame, they instead ran into "Professor

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:00.360
Hulk."

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:06.140
The long-awaited union of the two personalities
was the result of 18 months of lab-based gamma

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:09.110
exposure, leading to a perfect combination
of Banner and Hulk.

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:11.560
"I put the brains and the brawn together.

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:12.980
And now look at me.

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:15.260
Best of both worlds."

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:20.000
While the transformative scene itself was
originally meant for Infinity War, it ended

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:23.980
up being the perfect way to launch the third
and final part of the Hulk's arc.

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:28.940
As Endgame plays out, so too does the third
and final portion of Hulk's three-part-story

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:29.440
arc.

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:33.220
Looking back, it's hard to believe that this
is the same character who, just a handful

00:08:33.220 --> 00:08:37.760
of years earlier, had maintained a successful
gladiatorial career half a galaxy away.

00:08:37.780 --> 00:08:41.240
By Endgame, Hulk is refined, thoughtful, and
caring.

00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:45.540
He even watches his past self smashing his
way through New York City and declares it

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:46.380
"gratuitous."

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:50.640
The Banner part of his intellect allows the
Hulk character to finally interact as a normal

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:51.780
adult human being.

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:56.020
He's instrumental in convincing Thor to stop
sulking and pick up Stormbreaker for another

00:08:56.020 --> 00:08:57.020
go at Thanos.

00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:01.500
He's also able to process the loss of Black
Widow without causing much physical damage.

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:06.060
Most important of all, however, it's Hulk
who tracks down the Ancient One and convinces

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:07.760
her to surrender the Time Stone.

00:09:08.250 --> 00:09:12.560
Consequently, Hulk is also the only one shown
the negative effects that stealing the Infinity

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:13.940
Stones would have on time.

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:17.980
This places the responsibility squarely in
his hands to make sure everything gets put

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:20.340
back in place after the Avengers' victory.

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:24.880
While the third portion of Hulk's three-movie
story arc is packed with some great stuff,

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:28.740
there's one thing that stands out from all
of the rest: reversing Thanos' snap.

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:33.220
Ultimately, it ends up being Hulk and Hulk
alone who wields Stark's Infinity Gauntlet

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:34.880
and brings everyone back from the dead.

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:39.760
It's an utterly selfless move, and one that
Hulk wouldn't have volunteered for at any

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:40.880
other time in his life.

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:45.560
The added fact that the radiation emanating
from the gauntlet is "mostly gamma" lends

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:49.480
a pleasing touch of destiny to the act as
he takes the gauntlet and snaps his fingers.

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:52.640
To put it in his own words, it's like he was
"made for this."

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:57.560
It's a triumphant and breathtakingly worthy
way to end a story that began all the way

00:09:57.560 --> 00:09:59.840
back at the end of Avengers: Age of Ultron.

